  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Trending humid with heavy downpours and \nlocalized flash flooding possible into the weekend\n\nWPC continues to outlook most of the CWA in the MRGL risk of\nExcessive Rainfall (Flash Flooding) for the next 5 days. Seems\nlogical since we expect the dewpoints to rise into the 70s over\nthe S and U60s in the N and hang there for many days. PWATs seem\nto peak today around 2.25\" in the SE. Then they don't get that\nhigh again (daily peaks of 2\" in the SE, but 1.7\" in the NW). A\npattern shift to a more-zonal flow aloft should occur over the \nweekend, but a cold front and/or dry air is not in store for us.\n\nThe confidence in flooding is low due to confidence in \nplacement of the heaviest rain being low. Certainly, the wettest\nplaces from over the weekend will sit higher on the list, and \nthere are some hints that the SErn third or so of the area will \nbe most at- risk for repeated shots of heavy rain. But, no flood\nwatch are expected for today, and we'll only get a better feel \nfor each new day as we see where the previous day's precip has \nbeen heaviest.\n\n   ------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Severe thunderstorms are possible Thursday and \nFriday afternoons/evenings\n\nMRGL risk for severe wind gusts exist on Thurs (and likely\nFriday, too) over the SE half of the area per the latest SPC \noutlooks (new DY3 not out at press time). The shear is less than\nmodest, but CAPE does climb into the 1000-2000 J/kg each of\nthose aftns. However, the CAPE is long/skinny, which is more\nindicative of heavy rain threat. If we can get some dry air in\nthe mid levels either day, and the small height falls on Friday\ncan help jack up the instability and DCAPEs, any of our storms\ncould make a strong to severe gust. \n \n   ------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Heat index could approach 100F Thursday and \nFriday SE of Harrisburg\n\nJust going to start to mention this possibility here in the AFD\nfor now. The heat index does get very close to and may exceed\n100 degrees using the current NBM guidance temps/dews. There is\nsome doubt in the mind of an old forecaster, though, as he \nwonders if the anticipated clouds and showers/storms will keep \nthe HI lower - even if just a degree or two which would make the\ndifference in advy/no. The temps won't be all that abnormal, it\nwill be the dewpoints which would take us over. At this point,\nwe'll hold off on heat advisories.",
